          %title Propagators
          %h2#Propagators Propagators
          %p In this chapter we discuss the following topics:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em Background
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %em
                      %a{:href => "#FourierAnalysis"} Fourier analysis and Plane wave modes
                %li
                  %p
                    %em
                      %a{:href => "#MicrolocalAnalysisAndUltravioletDivergence"} Microlocal analysis and UV-Divergences
                %li
                  %p
                    %em
                      %a{:href => "#CauchyPrincipalValues"} Cauchy principal values
            %li
              %p
                %em Propagators for the free scalar field on Minkowski spacetime
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %em
                      %a{:href => "#AdvancedAndRetardedPropagatorsForKleinGordonEquationOnMinkowskiSpacetime"} advanced and regarded propagators
                %li
                  %p
                    %em
                      %a{:href => "#CausalPropagatorForKleinGordonEquationOnMinkowskiSpacetime"} causal propagator
                %li
                  %p
                    %em
                      %a{:href => "#HadamardPropagatorForKleinGordonOnMinkowskiSpacetime"} Wightman propagator
                %li
                  %p
                    %em
                      %a{:href => "#FeynmanPropagator"} Feynman propagator
                %li
                  %p
                    %em
                      %a{:href => "#WaveFrontSetsOfPropagatorsForKleinGordonOperatorOnMinkowskiSpacetime"} singular support and wave front sets
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "#DiracEquationOnMinkowskiSpacetimePropagators"} Propagators for the Dirac field on Minkowski spacetime
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            In the
            %a{:href => "#PhaseSpace"} previous chapter
            we have seen the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            (prop.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CovariantPhaseSpace"}
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theories"} Lagrangian field theories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/on-shell"} on-shell
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/space+of+field+histories"} space of field histories
            equipped with the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+form"} presymplectic form
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/transgression+of+variational+differential+forms"} transgressed
            from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+current"} presymplectic current
            of the theory; and we have seen that in good cases this induces a bilinear pairing on sufficiently well-behaved
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            (def.
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PoissonBracketOnHamiltonianLocalObservables"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal+symmetries"} infinitesimal symmetries
            of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/presymplectic+current"} presymplectic current
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            is of central importance for passing to actual
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %em
              %a{:href => "#Quantization"} Quantization
            below, it is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/infinitesimal"} infinitesimal
            approximation to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian field theory
          %p
            We have moreover seen that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            on the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/covariant+phase+space"} covariant phase space
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green+hyperbolic+differential+equation"} Green hyperbolic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            – the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peierls-Poisson+bracket"} Peierls-Poisson bracket
            – is determined by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+kernel"} integral kernel
            of the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+Green+function"} causal Green function
            (prop.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PPeierlsBracket"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+solution+of+a+PDE"} generalized solutions
            to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            (theorem
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#LinearObservablesForGreeFreeFieldTheoryAreDistributionalSolutionsToTheEquationsOfMotion"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+kernel"} integral kernel
            may be understood as
            %em propagating
            the values of an off-shell observable through
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PPeierlsBracket"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+kernel"} integral kernel
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+Green+function"} causal Green function
            is also called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
            (prop.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GreenFunctionsAreContinuous"}
          %p
            This means that for
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Green+hyperbolic+differential+equation"} Green hyperbolic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lagrangian+field+theory"} Lagrangian field theory
            the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Poisson+bracket"} Poisson bracket
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of the theory, is all encoded in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagators"} propagators
          %p
            The main tool for these computations is
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier+transform"} Fourier analysis
            (reviewed
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#FourierAnalysis"} below
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+histories"} field histories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagators"} propagators
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            are decomposed as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superpositions"} superpositions
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane+waves"} plane waves
            of various
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frequencies"} frequencies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+lengths"} wave lengths
            and
            = succeed "-" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+vector"} wave vector
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direction+of+a+vector"} direction
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagators"} propagators
            are exhibited as those
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/superpositions"} superpositions
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane+waves"} plane waves
            which satisfy the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dispersion+relation"} dispersion relation
            of the given
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equation+of+motion"} equation of motion
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plane+wave"} plane wave
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frequency"} frequency
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+length"} wave length
          %p
            This way the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
            is naturally decomposed into its contribution from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/positive+real+number"} positive
            and from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/negative+real+number"} negative
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frequencies"} frequencies
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
            is called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+propagator"} Wightman propagator
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#StandardHadamardDistributionOnMinkowskiSpacetime"}
            below). It turns out (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#SkewSymmetricPartOfHadmrdPropagatorIsCausalPropagatorForKleinGordonEquationOnMinkowskiSpacetime"}
            below) that this is equivalently the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sum"} sum
            of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#CausalPropagatorIsSkewSymmetric"}
            below), with a symmetric component, or equivalently that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
            is the skew-symmetrization of the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+propagator"} Wightman propagator
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
            discussed
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#FreeQuantumFields"} further below
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/2-point+function"} correlation function
            between two point-evaluation field observables (example
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PointEvaluationObservables"}
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vacuum+state"} vacuum state
            of the field theory (a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
            in the sense of def.
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#States"}
          %p
            Moreover, by def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AdvancedAndRetardedGreenFunctions"}
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
            also decomposes into its contributions with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/future"} future
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/past"} past
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/support+of+a+distribution"} support
            %em difference
            between the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/advanced+and+retarded+propagators"} advanced and retarded propagators
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#AdvancedRetardedPropagatorsForKleinGordonOnMinkowskiSpacetime"}
            below.
          %p
            Combining these two decompositions of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
            (positive/negative frequency as well as positive/negative time) yields one more propagator, the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#FeynmanPropagatorForKleinGordonEquationOnMinkowskiSpacetime"}
            below).
          %p
            We will see
            %a{:href => "#Quantization"} below
            that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
            is given by a “
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star+product"}> star product
            ” (on
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/observables"} observables
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/exponential"} exponentiating
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagators"} propagators
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagators"} propagators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+functions"} generalized functions
            (prop.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DistributionsAreGeneralizedFunctions"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagators"} propagators
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions"} distributions
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singularities"} singularities
            = succeed "\u201D" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV+divergences"} UV divergences
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Fourier+transform+of+distributions"} Fourier transforms
            (remark
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#UltravioletDivergencesFromPaleyWiener"}
            below) are absent (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H%C3%B6rmander%27s+criterion"}> Hörmander's criterion
            ”, prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HoermanderCriterionForProductOfDistributions"}
            below). These
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV+divergences"} UV divergences
            are captured by what what is called the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+front+set"} wave front set
            (def.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#WaveFrontSet"}
            below).
          %p
            The study of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/UV+divergences"} UV divergences
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributions"} distributions
            via their
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+front+sets"} wave front sets
            is called
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/microlocal+analysis"} microlocal analysis
            and provides powerful tools for the understanding of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagation+of+singularities+theorem"} propagation of singularities theorem
            (prop.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#PropagationOfSingularitiesTheorem"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/distributional+solutions+to+a+PDE"} distributional solutions
            (def.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DistributionalDerivatives"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Euler-Lagrange+equation"} Euler-Lagrange
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equations+of+motion"} equations of motion
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagators"} propagators
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/singular+support"} singular support
            propagates itself through
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spacetime"} spacetime
            along the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+front+set"} wave front set
          %p
            Using this theorem we work out the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/wave+front+sets"} wave front sets
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propagators"} propagators
            (prop.
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#WaveFronSetsForKGPropagatorsOnMinkowski"}
            below). Via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/H%C3%B6rmander%27s+criterion"} Hörmander's criterion
            (prop.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#HoermanderCriterionForProductOfDistributions"}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wightman+propagator"} Wightman propagator
            replaces the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/causal+propagator"} causal propagator
            in the construction of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Wick+algebra"} Wick algebra
            of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+field+theory"} free field theory
            (discussed below in
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#FreeQuantumFields"} Free quantum fields
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+propagator"} Feynman propagator
            similarly controls the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+observables"} quantum observables
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interaction"} interacting
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            (below in
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#FeynmanDiagrams"} Feynman diagrams
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \,
          %p
            The following table summarizes the structure of the system of propagators. (The column “as vacuum expectation value of field operators” will be discussed further below in
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "#FreeQuantumFields"} Free quantum fields
